---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { bookingHref } from '../data/site.js';

const bring = [
  'Every cream, tablet, oil or serum you are using — including anything bought without a prescription',
  'Previous prescriptions, even old ones',
  'Any blood tests, swabs or biopsy reports',
  'Photographs of the problem at its worst, if it fluctuates',
  'Roughly when it started, and what has changed since',
];

const patterns = [
  ['Treated repeatedly for a fungal infection that never clears', 'Frequently it was never fungal, or it is fungal but has been altered by a steroid cream. A KOH mount settles it.'],
  ['Hair fall treated with supplements for a year', 'Often nobody tested ferritin or thyroid function, and nobody examined the scalp under magnification.'],
  ['Acne cleared and then came back worse', 'Commonly a steroid-containing cream, or an antibiotic used alone for too long without a retinoid.'],
  ['Pigmentation that got darker after treatment', 'Usually laser or peel settings too aggressive for Indian skin, or sunscreen stopped once it improved.'],
  ['A rash treated as eczema that never settles', 'Worth reconsidering the diagnosis — fungal infection, contact allergy and psoriasis all get treated as eczema.'],
];
---

<Page
  title="Second opinions"
  description="Already been treated two or three times without improvement? That is most of this practice. Second opinions in clinic or online, across India."
  slug="second-opinions"
  intro="If you have been treated two or three times without improvement, the problem is usually the diagnosis rather than the treatment."
>
  <p>
    Most people who come here have already seen someone. Often several people. They arrive with a
    bag of creams, a condition that keeps returning, and a reasonable suspicion that nobody has
    actually worked out what is wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is not an unusual case here — it is the ordinary one. And the pattern is consistent
    enough to be worth naming: when a skin condition has resisted three rounds of treatment, the
    issue is far more often a diagnosis that was never established than a condition that is
    genuinely untreatable.
  </p>

  <h2>What usually turns out to be going on</h2>

  <table>
    <tbody>
      {
        patterns.map(([what, why]) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" style="width:42%">{what}</th>
            <td>{why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What a second opinion here involves</h2>

  <p>
    The first job is to establish what the condition actually is, which means going back to the
    beginning rather than adding another cream to the sequence. That means a proper history — how
    long, what changed before it started, what has been applied and what happened each time — and
    then examination, with the test that confirms or excludes the likely diagnosis.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sometimes that produces a different diagnosis. Sometimes it confirms that the earlier
    diagnosis was right and the treatment simply needed longer or a higher dose, which is a
    useful answer too, and it saves you starting over again elsewhere.
  </p>

  <p>
    What you should expect either way is to be told what the condition is, how confident that
    assessment is, what the plan is, and roughly how long it should take — in language you can
    repeat to someone else.
  </p>

  <h2>Bring this with you</h2>

  <ul>{bring.map((b) => <li>{b}</li>)}</ul>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Especially the creams</h3>
    <p>
      Combination creams containing a potent steroid are sold widely in India for almost any itch
      or rash. They are the single commonest reason a straightforward condition becomes a
      difficult one, and they change how it looks so the diagnosis is missed. Bring the tube
      rather than the name — the ingredient list is what matters.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Can this be done online?</h2>

  <p>
    Often, yes — and for a second opinion it is frequently the more practical option, because the
    history and the previous prescriptions carry a lot of the information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where the answer depends on dermoscopy, a KOH mount or a biopsy, you will be told that at the
    start of the consultation rather than afterwards, and advised where to be seen.
  </p>

  <div class="actions" style="margin-top:28px">
    <a
      class="btn btn-primary"
      href="/consult-online/"
      data-track="cta_clicked"
      data-label="consult_online"
      data-placement="second_opinion_page">Get a second opinion online</a
    >
    <a
      class="btn btn-secondary"
      href={bookingHref({ concern: 'Second opinion' })}
      rel="noopener"
      data-track="booking_started"
      data-mode="clinic"
      data-placement="second_opinion_page">Book at a clinic</a
    >
  </div>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/#diagnosis">How a diagnosis is made</a></li>
    <li><a href="/conditions/">All conditions</a></li>
    <li><a href="/consult-online/">How online consultation works</a></li>
  </ul>
</Page>
